WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.240
An invisible mass equivalent to tens of thousands of galaxies is dragging the milky way and its neighbors across space at 600 kilometers per second. This is Fascinating Frontiers, episode 195. The great attractor sits behind the Milky Way's dense central plane. It lies hidden in the zone of avoidance where optical telescopes cannot penetrate the dust. X-ray and infrared observations have identified the Norma cluster as one contributor to the pole.

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:34.320
The more distant chaply supercluster adds further gravitational influence.

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:40.060
Even combined, these structures do not fully account for the observed motion of the local group.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:45.120
The remaining mass may lie in still unmapped filaments of the cosmic web.

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:51.320
How much additional unseen structure is required to explain the full velocity remains an open measurement.

00:00:52.220 --> 00:00:55.340
Now shifting to new findings at much smaller scales.

00:00:55.340 --> 00:01:00.800
The image reveals the smallest known brown dwarfs, some only twice the mass of Jupiter.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:06.520
Astronomers use the telescope to search the region and identify these objects at the lower end

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:08.400
of the mass range for brown dwarfs.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:16.180
The findings extend the study of how such objects form and how the star formation process operates at planetary masses.

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:20.520
A few proto-stars appear in the upper right portion of the panorama,

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:25.560
A jetting jets that collide with surrounding gas and dust to create bright, her big,

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:26.500
horror objects.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:31.280
One of the newly identified brown dwarfs shows signs of a salounding disc.

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:36.600
The detection suggests that planet formation could occur around objects whose total mass

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:38.500
is comparable to that of a planet.

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:42.940
On a different note, China continues expanding its orbital fleets.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:50.500
A yinli-1 rocket lifted off at 2200 UTC on September 15th from a barge in the East China

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:58.440
The launch deployed 8 Tianfan Degui 26 Zhu satellites, along with the EU-HT-Gishu Shion

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:00.960
Weising Experimental Internet Satellite.

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:08.980
The barge launch site sits near coordinates 123.7 degrees east 31.2 degrees north in the

00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:09.920
east China Sea.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:16.420
Separately, the Chinese Zhu Chwek IIE rocket placed the space-sale satellite into polar orbit.

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:21.720
the mission adds another spacecraft to the growing Chinese commercial remote sensing fleet.

00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:28.900
Next up, SpaceX is preparing its next major test flight. SpaceX has set an earliest launch date

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:35.240
of September 22 for the first attempt to reach full orbit with Starship. The mission remains subject

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:41.400
to regulatory approval before liftoff. Engineers plan a conservative approach that will attempt orbital

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:45.860
circularization only after confirming all systems are performing nominally.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:51.640
The flight represents the first-time starship will target a complete orbital profile, rather

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:53.140
than a sub-orbital trajectory.

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:59.520
SpaceX president Gwynne Shotwell addressed questions about NASA's reliance on the Dragon spacecraft.

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:03.060
She stated that the vehicle is not retiring today.

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:08.740
Dragon has operated for six years and remains the primary crewed spacecraft available to

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:10.200
NASA and other customers.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:15.100
the spacecraft continues to support both cargo and crew rotations to the

00:03:15.100 --> 00:03:20.000
International Space Station. NASA outlined a step-by-step sequence of missions

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:24.220
that will establish the first lunar base. The plan includes incremental

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:28.500
deliveries of infrastructure and scientific instruments ahead of sustained human

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:33.620
presence. Early phases emphasize robotic precursors and resource utilization

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:38.420
demonstrations. Later stages will integrate crewed landings and long duration

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:44.760
Habitat modules. Beijing announced the donation of Cheng'a-6 lunar sample material to the United

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:50.160
Nations. The sample originates from the far side of the Moon and will be made available for

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:56.280
shared scientific study. The Cheng'a-6 mission returned material from a previously unsampled region

00:03:56.280 --> 00:04:01.800
of the lunar surface. The donation was framed as a gesture supporting international cooperation

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:08.100
and multilateralism in space exploration. NASA is preparing two public engagement events

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:13.720
focused on the moon and future technologies. The annual International Observe the Moon Night

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:19.800
invites public participation in lunar observing activities worldwide. NASA coordinates the night

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:25.360
to encourage widespread viewing of the moon and related educational programming. Observers are

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:30.520
encouraged to use telescopes, binoculars, or even the unaided eye during the coordinated evening.

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:36.420
A separate November event at Kennedy Space Center will showcase new aircraft technologies,

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:42.260
autonomous vehicles and other innovations. Attendees can meet the teams developing these systems

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:48.160
and explore future transportation concepts. Charged particles interacting with the upper atmosphere

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:53.840
produce a distinctive purple aurora display. The image captures the aurora's interaction with

00:04:53.840 --> 00:05:00.160
Earth's magnetic field and atmospheric gases. Such displays occur when solar wind particles excite

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:06.220
oxygen and nitrogen at specific altitudes. The purple coloration arises from a particular mix

00:05:06.220 --> 00:05:09.860
of emission lines visible under certain geomagnetic conditions.

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:15.620
Three robots operated together in a field test to evaluate their ability to function as an

00:05:15.620 --> 00:05:21.220
independent science team. Researchers assessed how the fleet could coordinate observations and

00:05:21.220 --> 00:05:27.140
decision-making during future exploration missions. The test examined autonomous task allocation

00:05:27.140 --> 00:05:33.700
and data sharing protocols among the machines. Pilot Mike Lippert powered up the X-59 cockpit

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:36.880
it and ran the engines on the ground for the first time this summer.

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:42.320
The run forms part of his preparation to fly the experimental quiet supersonic aircraft

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:43.980
for NASA's Quest mission.

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:49.000
Lippert noted that the cockpit layout felt comfortable during the initial systems checkout.

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:54.780
The ground test marks a key milestone before the aircraft advances to taxi and flight phases.

00:05:55.500 --> 00:06:00.620
Department of the Air Force Secretary Troy Mike stated publicly that the United States currently

00:06:00.620 --> 00:06:02.260
maintains weapons in orbit.

00:06:02.260 --> 00:06:07.940
it. The announcement came during the 2026 Air and Space Cyber Conference, an addressed

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:13.840
modernization plans against AI-enabled threats. The statement represents the first official

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:19.600
confirmation of operational on-orbit weapon systems. Our local group travels at roughly

00:06:19.600 --> 00:06:25.440
600 kilometers per second relative to the cosmic microwave background. That speed would carry

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:30.500
a commercial jet from New York to Los Angeles in under seven seconds if sustained in the same

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:36.220
direction. The motion is not produced by the expansion of space itself, it is a peculiar

00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:41.020
velocity caused by the uneven distribution of matter on scales of hundreds of millions

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:45.000
of light years. Picture riding alongside a photon leaving the

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:50.740
shapely supercluster. Every time it passes a denser filament or cluster, the photon gains

00:06:50.740 --> 00:06:56.500
a tiny gravitational redshift or blueshift that cumulatively maps the mass concentrations

00:06:56.500 --> 00:07:01.720
pulling us forward. The great attractor region alone contains the equivalent of tens of

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:07.540
thousands of Milky Way masses. Yet even that falls short of the total gravitational tug measured

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:13.800
from the microwave dipole. The remaining deficit points to vast, still unseen walls and filaments

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:18.980
lying deeper in the zone of avoidance. We can measure the velocity vector to high precision,

00:07:19.360 --> 00:07:25.180
but we cannot yet see the full mass distribution responsible for it. Keep an eye on the next Starship

00:07:25.180 --> 00:07:30.360
launch window as regulatory reviews continue. That's fascinating frontiers for today.

